node_modules/
TestResults/
*.received.*

# `dotnet run -- schema export` writes this next to the SDL; the SDL is the
# artifact we track, the settings dump is not.
*-settings.json

# Chapter 7 composes this from samples/federated-wire/graph.yaml. It is a
# build artifact of two committed schemas, not a source file.
#
# federation/supergraph.json is the same kind of artifact and is committed
# anyway, which is a deliberate difference rather than an oversight: chapter 9
# prints what is inside it, so it has to be a file a reader can open, and
# verify.ps1 recomposes and compares. Chapter 7 prints nothing out of its one.
samples/federated-wire/supergraph.json

# Chapter 11's sample, for the same reason: the chapter prints query plans the
# router built from this file and never the file itself. scripts/entity-cases.mjs
# composes a copy of its own anyway, routed at ports no launch profile uses.
samples/entity-resolution/supergraph.json

# Chapter 13's sample, same reason again. What that chapter prints out of this
# graph is the composed client schema and a query plan, both read back through
# the router rather than out of the file.
samples/interface-object/supergraph.json

# Chapter 9's lab composes here, so that a reader trying things out never has
# git status telling them they have changed the committed config.
federation/scratch.json
